#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175756 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d600416b-05f5-593f-a2ad-bb3a08d60b45" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Inhomogeneous ODE to First-Order System"}] :question/id #uuid "98209a93-97d9-5068-bfb4-34c124bd2333" :question/math-academy-id "e-9042" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\ny''  - 7t y' + e^{t}y= -t\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$ and $x_2(t) = y'(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t y' - e^{t}y - t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t x_2 -e^{t}x_1 - t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=7t{x}_{2}-{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = -e^{t}x_1 + 7t x_2 - t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175769 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7f2a0b-777f-5d63-9f3a-3b3f4a6969d8" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Inhomogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "f17360c7-9edb-50ec-b9d8-d8baad76a262" :question/math-academy-id "e-9139" :question/problem "Consider the third-order differential equation\n\n$$\ny''' + t y' - 8 y = 1 - t^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $y = y(t).$ If\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the given differential equation can be rewritten as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf f(t),$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/2b3ba6edd838ee516ad902008726afbca516ab15892aad0f236c5a2270261985.png)\nDetermine the matrices $A$ and $\\mathbf{f}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′} & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' = - t y' + 8 y + 1 - t^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' = - t x_2 + 8 x_1 + 1 - t^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=8{x}_{1}-t{x}_{2}+1-{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)$ as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/5a343acd288c1f6ddc72320b4eb17d9f24d3d41adebec579e70b9ff7ba17d0bb.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/dcb089aa64d2aeb959e6b33b03afecef75de96962a0468eb31b590ed6ecad6eb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d600416b-05f5-593f-a2ad-bb3a08d60b45" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Inhomogeneous ODE to First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82760ffd-6fde-5b2d-bc67-c7429a28c87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{t}x_{2} - x_{3} + t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173527} {:answer/id #uuid "8e30afae-4de5-5913-893b-c6f88af2ef58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{1} - \\sqrt{t}x_{2} + t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173528} {:answer/id #uuid "922bdfed-e539-5811-8344-f257de7c1f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{1} + \\sqrt{t}x_{3} + t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173529} {:answer/id #uuid "b7040b19-aee0-5538-993b-df1eaf5dd833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{t}x_{2} + x_{3} + t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173530} {:answer/id #uuid "00ef6877-32dd-5dbc-a3af-7a916ebf5d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x_{1} - \\sqrt{t}x_{3} - t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "922bdfed-e539-5811-8344-f257de7c1f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{1} + \\sqrt{t}x_{3} + t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173529} :answer-field/id #uuid "c28961f7-8cc1-5f29-a1d8-140658913d7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd48b46d-ec29-5080-9e57-ded7b691472a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107932" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}-y={t}^{2}\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},$ ${x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}+y+{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}+{x}_{1}+{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={x}_{1}+\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}+{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t)={x}_{1}+\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}+{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d600416b-05f5-593f-a2ad-bb3a08d60b45" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Inhomogeneous ODE to First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d796322-0c5f-5c6e-bfdb-4e26209cf6e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}{x}_{1}+t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367530} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d82c67-3abf-50e3-912b-fcefb565e698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{x}_{1}+{t}^{2}{x}_{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367531} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fa888b-ed5e-5ac7-be9c-2d0bce87ac3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367532} {:answer/id #uuid "a2375be9-a6fb-59e6-818f-153a51a58ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}{x}_{2}+t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367533} {:answer/id #uuid "47d798b8-9ab9-5692-a4aa-24cf1cea6eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t{x}_{1}+{t}^{2}{x}_{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2375be9-a6fb-59e6-818f-153a51a58ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}{x}_{2}+t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367533} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a9c34b3-4337-5630-a530-d5430581a4fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5a32215-b5b1-50a0-91d4-9a509b256a6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109534" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{t}^{2}{y}^{′}=t+1\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={t}^{2}{y}^{′}+t+1.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={t}^{2}{x}_{2}+t+1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={t}^{2}{x}_{2}+t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t)={t}^{2}{x}_{2}+t+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d600416b-05f5-593f-a2ad-bb3a08d60b45" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Inhomogeneous ODE to First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f6cd687-7442-58ed-8ae0-6bab4649fb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t{x}_{1}-{t}^{2}{x}_{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367537} {:answer/id #uuid "9e90cb3c-7e3a-5c7d-9fe0-a113d3a1fa71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}{x}_{1}-3t{x}_{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367538} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5a835a-6dfc-575c-a0cf-1f74da36c392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}{x}_{1}+3t{x}_{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367539} {:answer/id #uuid "d39b962f-b144-5e14-82e7-e80c6ffbbfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}{x}_{1}-3t{x}_{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367540} {:answer/id #uuid "f37b47ab-be49-53c4-b2a9-322c22491ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3t{x}_{1}+{t}^{2}{x}_{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e5a835a-6dfc-575c-a0cf-1f74da36c392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}{x}_{1}+3t{x}_{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367539} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f37352a-9a04-535f-bb88-a6601ffedb84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b8dd8ee-7cc2-5fd2-bcd2-b8827398104f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109538" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+{t}^{2}y=6\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3t{y}^{′}-{t}^{2}y+6.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3t{x}_{2}-{t}^{2}{x}_{1}+6.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-{t}^{2}{x}_{1}+3t{x}_{2}+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t)=-{t}^{2}{x}_{1}+3t{x}_{2}+6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d600416b-05f5-593f-a2ad-bb3a08d60b45" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Inhomogeneous ODE to First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64fdadbb-e982-5479-9580-2c57c819459b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}_{1}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{x}_{3}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367544} {:answer/id #uuid "20fbfff6-bec8-5f88-8ce0-3a78cf21c8a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367545} {:answer/id #uuid "77f21c97-4c5f-5c08-9e5e-ea41420abf9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367546} {:answer/id #uuid "d98f4250-d9bc-5a16-b6a0-acf0886a28aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367547} {:answer/id #uuid "c2650ba0-4101-56c3-a14c-8930e8f1667c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77f21c97-4c5f-5c08-9e5e-ea41420abf9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367546} :answer-field/id #uuid "67a7166f-b533-57c3-b077-ceac06c62f6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "511f3a0a-b968-54ba-a869-530d4a63e749" :question/math-academy-id "q-109547" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}-{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,y=2t\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},$ ${x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}={y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,y+2t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}={x}_{3}-3{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+2t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d600416b-05f5-593f-a2ad-bb3a08d60b45" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Inhomogeneous ODE to First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3222640d-b8d0-55f9-bb1d-5014ca0c762b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}_{1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}+6t{x}_{3}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367551} {:answer/id #uuid "2efc4051-8615-56f4-9dd4-459d199339bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+6t{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367552} {:answer/id #uuid "728b45bd-ec6c-5c44-b600-a7f0813bb9a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-6t{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367553} {:answer/id #uuid "53508aef-8e7e-56da-b01c-e1cbbda69a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{x}_{1}-6t{x}_{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367554} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1b7e0c-cb7f-5ef7-a5b9-df21cf5d787f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}_{1}+6t{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f1b7e0c-cb7f-5ef7-a5b9-df21cf5d787f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}_{1}+6t{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367555} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66720c1-106d-5f08-81ee-2b6e172ca5da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de8bfde6-0de1-55c1-b649-65cf4f0baf96" :question/math-academy-id "q-109552" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{y}^{″}-6t{y}^{′}-9y=t\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},$ ${x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{y}^{″}+6t{y}^{′}+9y+t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}+6t{x}_{2}+9{x}_{1}+t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=9{x}_{1}+6t{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t)=9{x}_{1}+6t{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}+t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7f2a0b-777f-5d63-9f3a-3b3f4a6969d8" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Inhomogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8c55125-804c-5078-bd1b-80801003ea88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367556} {:answer/id #uuid "f086a194-5df0-5d5f-aef8-132e597a919a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8c55125-804c-5078-bd1b-80801003ea88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367556} :answer-field/id #uuid "9371147f-12d5-5180-b2c8-80b67fc263c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174872} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f566e5-7a73-5351-b3fb-364862c5482f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0f566e5-7a73-5351-b3fb-364862c5482f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367558} :answer-field/id #uuid "d211fe97-5680-5762-a1da-bfddb28e5bd1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174873} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adb2791e-50e5-58ce-82cf-fdda1b83ac90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adb2791e-50e5-58ce-82cf-fdda1b83ac90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367560} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1eec381-4c91-58a8-a8ca-6c06885b283a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e2580a-ec49-5116-914c-ffa6ada73823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30e2580a-ec49-5116-914c-ffa6ada73823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367559} :answer-field/id #uuid "35aa6f99-4d2b-5876-bcff-17eceab288eb" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0817c0f5-76d4-5e96-ac95-64985ef69d50" :question/math-academy-id "q-321304" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $1$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-6{y}^{′}+y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-6{x}_{2}+{x}_{1}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7f2a0b-777f-5d63-9f3a-3b3f4a6969d8" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Inhomogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81aaa12f-5f44-5e1b-9d33-289e01c35fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81aaa12f-5f44-5e1b-9d33-289e01c35fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367563} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7892b1b-8eed-51f2-9b69-6876ebd9ea00" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94f4de90-19ee-555e-9335-e0dbb6f15b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94f4de90-19ee-555e-9335-e0dbb6f15b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367565} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce75c77f-09a9-531d-a66b-fe8d5144eeaf" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f82fa6de-2b80-50dd-8ed0-74329d168b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/v