b/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8b0c86d-960e-5325-8cbf-b1930fe42d93" :question/math-academy-id "q-321369" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}-t{y}^{″}+{e}^{t}{y}^{′}=2$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |  | $,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | $-{e}^{t}$ | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t),$ ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t),$ and ${y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=t{y}^{″}-{e}^{t}{y}^{′}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=t{x}_{3}-{e}^{t}{x}_{2}+2.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-{e}^{t}{x}_{2}+t{x}_{3}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{t} & t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7f2a0b-777f-5d63-9f3a-3b3f4a6969d8" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Inhomogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c36ec414-cb4e-5f35-a5e2-2904e8085024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c36ec414-cb4e-5f35-a5e2-2904e8085024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367569} :answer-field/id #uuid "b26adaae-17dd-575a-a4a9-1f0cfa9518d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7afa87b8-4bfd-595c-83d7-8b9ffa732757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7afa87b8-4bfd-595c-83d7-8b9ffa732757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367571} :answer-field/id #uuid "8222deeb-075c-50f0-b697-72022b9d3e53" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367570} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447cb29b-4655-55f3-be33-870571f78f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "447cb29b-4655-55f3-be33-870571f78f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367573} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd315805-0856-54fb-9201-e4a368ee52b2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367572} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ab13c9c-6b3e-5190-b9fb-8c99b9aebf4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ab13c9c-6b3e-5190-b9fb-8c99b9aebf4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367575} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f05358-b962-52b2-a155-dc760c431450" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5022879-0d97-5b2b-9e04-945eabbafa5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-330741" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+3y={e}^{t}$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-3$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{y}^{′}-3y+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7f2a0b-777f-5d63-9f3a-3b3f4a6969d8" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Inhomogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27138069-06a0-50c5-8fd2-66c2ec8d67ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27138069-06a0-50c5-8fd2-66c2ec8d67ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367578} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaf02814-f6cf-55d7-9ee9-f92c9784572c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367577} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46d49d23-c188-5322-9704-7e99de35ea4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592